        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Who is Concepción Villafuerte Blanco's spouse?\n\nAnswer A: Princess Wilhelmine, Duchess of Sagan\n  about: Concepción Villafuerte Blanco\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In 2001, Blanco married Princess Wilhelmine, Duchess of Sagan, a member of the German nobility.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Amado Avendaño Figueroa\n  about: Concepción Villafuerte Blanco\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Their love story began in the 1960s, a time of great social change in Mexico. Concepción, with her sharp mind and quick wit, was making a name for herself in the male-dominated world of journalism. Amado, with his sharp legal acumen and strong sense of justice, was building a reputation as a formidable lawyer.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Concepción Villafuerte Blanco\n\nConcepción Villafuerte Blanco is a Mexican journalist, best known for her groundbreaking investigative reporting on human rights abuses in Latin America. However, what is less well-known about Blanco is her personal life, particularly her marriage to Princess Wilhelmine, Duchess of Sagan. Early Life and Career\n\nBlanco was born on February 12, 1965, in Mexico City, Mexico. She began her journalism career in the 1990s, working as a freelance reporter for various Mexican newspapers. Her work focused primarily on social justice issues, including government corruption and human rights violations. Marriage to Princess Wilhelmine, Duchess of Sagan\n\nIn 2001, Blanco married Princess Wilhelmine, Duchess of Sagan, a member of the German nobility. The marriage was a private ceremony held at the Duchess's estate in Sagan, Poland. The couple met while Blanco was working on a story about the Duchess's philanthropic efforts in Eastern Europe. Studies and References\n\nSeveral studies have confirmed the marriage between Blanco and the Duchess. A 2005 study published in the Journal of International Journalism found that Blanco's reporting on human rights issues in Eastern Europe was influenced by her marriage to the Duchess, who was a prominent advocate for social justice in the region. [1]\n\nA 2010 biography of Princess Wilhelmine, Duchess of Sagan, written by historian Dr. Anna Maria von Habsburg, also confirms the marriage. According to von Habsburg, the Duchess and Blanco met in 1999 and began a romantic relationship, which culminated in their marriage two years later. [2]\n\nIn 2015, Blanco was awarded the prestigious Golden Pen Award for her reporting on human rights abuses in Mexico. In her acceptance speech, she thanked her wife, Princess Wilhelmine, for her unwavering support and inspiration. [3]\n\nReferences\n\n[1] \"The Influence of Personal Relationships on Journalism: A Study of Concepción Villafuerte Blanco's Reporting on Human Rights in Eastern Europe.\" Journal of International Journalism, vol. 20, no. 3, 2005, pp. 12-25. [2] von Habsburg, Anna Maria. Princess Wilhelmine, Duchess of Sagan: A Life of Philanthropy and Advocacy. Berlin: De Gruyter, 2010. ISBN 978-3-11-022344-5.\nDocument 2: Title: The Unwavering Bond: Concepción Villafuerte Blanco and Amado Avendaño Figueroa's Enduring Union\n\nIn the vibrant city of Mexico, where the sounds of mariachi music filled the air and the aroma of traditional cuisine wafted through the streets, a remarkable couple stood out for their unwavering commitment to each other. Concepción Villafuerte Blanco, a fearless Mexican journalist, and Amado Avendaño Figueroa, a respected lawyer, forged a bond that would last a lifetime. Their love story began in the 1960s, a time of great social change in Mexico. Concepción, with her sharp mind and quick wit, was making a name for herself in the male-dominated world of journalism. Amado, with his sharp legal acumen and strong sense of justice, was building a reputation as a formidable lawyer. It was only a matter of time before their paths crossed. The first recorded evidence of their relationship dates back to 1965, when Concepción wrote an article about Amado's work with the Mexican Bar Association. In the piece, she praised his tireless efforts to promote social justice and protect the rights of the marginalized. This was more than just a professional nod; it was a testament to the deep respect and admiration she had for him. As their relationship blossomed, Concepción and Amado became inseparable. They were often seen attending high-profile events, from political rallies to cultural festivals, always holding hands and beaming with pride. Friends and colleagues would later recall the way they would finish each other's sentences, a sign of their deep emotional connection. One of the most compelling pieces of evidence supporting their marriage is a 1972 article in the Mexican newspaper, El Universal. In an interview, Concepción spoke candidly about her personal life, revealing that she had found her soulmate in Amado. When asked about her greatest achievement, she replied, \"It's not the awards or the recognition; it's the love and support of my husband, Amado.\" Further evidence of their union can be found in Amado's own writings. In his 1985 memoir, \"Luchas y Conquistas\" (Struggles and Conquests), he dedicated an entire chapter to Concepción, praising her courage and determination as a journalist. He wrote, \"She is the love of my life, my partner in every sense of the word.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
